============================================================================================
 (A) Eisenstein-norm check: Phi_6(n) = |n - zeta_6|^2, zeta_6 = (1+i sqrt3)/2  [Q(sqrt-3)]
============================================================================================
   n=3: |n - zeta_6|^2 = 7.0000   Phi_6(n) = 7   match=True
   n=7: |n - zeta_6|^2 = 43.0000   Phi_6(n) = 43   match=True
   n=14: |n - zeta_6|^2 = 183.0000   Phi_6(n) = 183   match=True

============================================================================================
 prime factors of Phi_6(n) are =1 mod 6 (Eisenstein SPLIT primes), except 3 (ramified):
============================================================================================
   n=3: Phi_6=7 -> primes [7]; classes [(7, '1 mod6')]
   n=7: Phi_6=43 -> primes [43]; classes [(43, '1 mod6')]
   n=14: Phi_6=183 -> primes [3, 61]; classes [(3, '3(ramified)'), (61, '1 mod6')]
   n=13: Phi_6=157 -> primes [157]; classes [(157, '1 mod6')]
   n=8: Phi_6=57 -> primes [3, 19]; classes [(3, '3(ramified)'), (19, '1 mod6')]
   => every prime p|Phi_6(n), p!=3, satisfies p=1 mod 6 (p splits in Z[zeta_6], the Eisenstein integers).

============================================================================================
 THE CONTENT (since the inequality is trivial): is n/Phi_6(n) the COVERING-MIN?
============================================================================================
   n=14: Phi_6=183=3*61 (61 = 1 mod 6, splits; 3 ramifies). q=13 is PRIME, so PG(2,13) EXISTS
   => a (183, 14, 1) SINGER difference set of 14 speeds mod 183 (every nonzero diff once).
   n/Phi_6 = 14/183 = 0.076503;  1/n = 1/14 = 0.071429;  margin = 0.005074
   The inequality 14/183 >= 1/14 is n>=1 (trivial). OPEN: is 14/183 the genuine covering-min,
   i.e. is the (183,14,1) projective-plane covering OPTIMAL (no covering beats it)? -- a
   design-theoretic optimality question, the Q(sqrt-3)/Eisenstein 'existence-column'.
